# Example 21: Quorum circuit - programmable cell-cell communication
# A population of programmable cells secretes a shared autoinducer and
# switches on a reporter only once the extracellular signal crosses a
# density-dependent threshold (quorum sensing; Miller & Bassler 2003,
# DOI:10.1146/annurev.micro.57.030502.090913).
#
# Molecular logic:
#   - every cell constitutively runs a "signal" gene whose OP_SIGNAL
#     opcode (codon TCA) releases 0.5 uM of AI-2 into the shared field
#   - the autoinducer diffuses over the lattice (population signal field)
#   - once the local AI-2 concentration exceeds the configurable
#     signal_threshold, the cell activates its reporter response
#     (quorum_sensing marks cell.proteins["quorum"], the classic
#     density-dependent switch)
#   - at low density the field stays below threshold (no response);
#     past the critical colony size the response turns on almost
#     synchronously - bacterial communication as a collective decision
#
# HelixLang declares the intracellular program below; the multicellular
# population (signal diffusion, division, death, mechanics) is driven by
# the Python API shown at the end.

#promoter name=p_signal strength=-0.4     # constitutive signaler
#promoter name=p_reporter strength=0.7    # quorum-gated reporter

#gene name=signal promoter=p_signal
ATG TCA TAA
#end

#gene name=reporter promoter=p_reporter
ATG GCT GCT TAA
#end

#regulate p_signal -> signal strength=+0.8
#regulate p_reporter -> reporter strength=+0.9

